# Always commit and push to `main` when done

When any task is complete, **always** commit the changes you made and **always** push to `origin main` before ending the turn, unless there is nothing to commit or push failed (report failures; never force-push). The user has standing approval — do not ask first.

## Scope: only commit your own changes

`git status` will often show pre-existing modifications from other work. **Only stage the specific files you edited or created in this session.** Use `git add <path1> <path2>` (never `git add .` or `git add -A`).

If unsure whether a stray modified file is yours, leave it alone.

## Commit message style

Match the existing repo style: a single-line subject in Title Case, no conventional-commit prefix, no body.

Recent examples from `git log`:

```
Platform fee: 1% of storage reward on every L1 Arweave upload
Move /about System Architecture next to Arweave + Stats
Reorder /about: move Platform Stats next to Fee Structure
```

This repo runs on **PowerShell**, not bash — do NOT use `cat <<'EOF'` heredocs (they fail with "The '<' operator is reserved for future use"). For single-line subjects, just use plain `-m`:

```powershell
git commit -m "Homepage: drop edge vignette overlay"
```

If you ever need a multi-line commit message, use a PowerShell here-string and pipe through a temp file, or use `-m` multiple times (one per paragraph).

## Push

```bash
git push origin main
```

If push fails (non-fast-forward, hook rejection, etc.) report the error — do not force-push.

## Never commit

- `.env*` files (other than `.env.example`)
- Anything containing a real `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN`, private key, or wallet seed
